  "query_preview": "..The weak MCV is continuing to move north-northeastward and showers\nand thunderstorms are pushing into Utah. Mostly clear skies are\nleading to showers and thunderstorms to develop along the higher \nterrain of the Rim, and we should start to see showers and \nthunderstorms over the White Mountains over the next couple of \nhours as well. Decent southerly flow is expected to push \ndeveloping showers and thunderstorms northward, which may limit \nthe threat for flash flooding from individual storms, but any \nback-building storms, caused by outflow from thunderstorms \npushing southward will create localized areas of higher risk for \nflash flooding through the afternoon and early evening hours. \nShower and thunderstorm activity should settle down tonight.\n\nFor Thursday and into the weekend, the high over New Mexico is \nexpected to move westward and strengthen, This slowly introduces \ndrier, mid-level air and subsidence, causing the Monsoon activity \nwill slow down. We don't expect a total stop, but it will be slow \nthrough the weekend. The high may return to a more favorable \nlocation by next week, with easterly steering flow returning, but \nthis outlook could change in a week and will be monitored. The \nrelocation and strengthening of the high, with the slow down of \nthe Monsoon, will cause temperatures to soar. This will cause many\nlocations to reach the warmest temperatures so far this year. \nExtreme heat warnings remain in effect for the Grand Canyon, \nYavapai and northern Gila Counties below 4000 ft, as well for \nMarble and Glen Canyon, including the city of Page, and Oak Creek \nand Sycamore Canyons, including the city of Sedona.",
